          "heading": "Charge carriers and band diagrams",
          "content": "# Unit 1, Lesson 1: Charge carriers and band diagrams\n\n**Model:** `n\u2248Nc exp(-(Ec-Ef)/(kT))`  \n**Outcome:** Analyze the operating principle, calculate a reference case, identify the limit of the approximation, and plan an independent check.\n\n## Physical mechanism\n\nA semiconductor contains mobile electrons in the conduction band and holes in the valence band. Fermi level is a statistical chemical potential and does not describe an individual electron trajectory. Band bending near a junction indicates an internal electrostatic field; it is not a literal slope in physical space. Doping moves carrier populations and modifies conductivity while preserving local charge neutrality away from depletion zones.\n\nDraw the relevant circuit or band diagram and name the source of energy, the signal that controls the result, the return path and the reference directions. A model of an individual element must be used with conservation of current and voltage around the complete circuit. This distinction is critical because a device characteristic measured under one set of bias conditions may not hold with a different load. Describe which parameters are external controls and which are intrinsic or empirical device parameters. In **semiconductor physics and circuit models**, an especially important issue is **material state**; make it explicit before calculating anything.\n\n## Derivation and calculation\n\nStart from `n\u2248Nc exp(-(Ec-Ef)/(kT))`. Explain the assumptions required to use the expression and distinguish DC current, incremental gain, instantaneous waveform, average power and RMS quantities wherever they arise. Write the associated Kirchhoff equations and translate all input data to compatible SI units. Verify that the final expression has the dimensions of the required output. If the expression is nonlinear, first identify the valid region; if it is an incremental result, identify the linearization point. An elegant equation is not proof that the circuit's bias or signal swing permits the result.\n\n**Worked example.** For equal mobilities and donor populations of 10^15 and 10^16 cm^-3 under full ionization, the electron part of conductivity changes by an approximate factor of ten. This proportionality fails if mobility changes with doping.\n\n**Two independent checks.** First examine the zero-drive or zero-load limit where physically applicable. Second change one stated parameter by 10% while holding the others fixed. Explain the sign of the resulting response from the device physics rather than exclusively from differentiation. If this relation predicts a very large current, voltage gain or output power, inspect the supply, load and dissipation limits. The provided numbers are illustrative and are not claims of verified measured devices.\n\n## Limitations and expected departures\n\nThe nondegenerate Boltzmann expression is unreliable for heavy degenerate doping and may require Fermi\u2013Dirac statistics.\n\nThis is the boundary of the teaching model, not evidence that all equations are unreliable. To judge model quality, identify the dominant neglected effect and whether it becomes important at high frequency, high drive, high temperature, or a specific operating region. Component spread may require using lower and upper device ratings rather than a single nominal value. Record whether an observed deviation is likely to arise from a different bias condition, instrument impedance or a real phenomenon the simple expression omits.\n\n## Experimental reasoning\n\nSketch equilibrium band edges and identify which gradient corresponds to an electric field.\n\nBefore energizing hardware, calculate expected terminal voltages and resistor power dissipation and confirm the fixture uses an isolated current-limited low-voltage supply. Measure from a defined ground and avoid exposing test equipment to uncontrolled potentials. Build a table of controlled inputs, model predictions, observed outputs and measurement uncertainty. A measured curve should have labelled axes and should reveal when the hypothesized relation stops being useful. A single matching value does not validate the whole operating range.\n\n## Design analysis and independent validation\n\nTreat **charge carriers and band diagrams** as a testable physical claim about **material state**. Before selecting components, define the source, load, reference arrows and nominal temperature. Then make two different predictions: one based on `n\u2248Nc exp(-(Ec-Ef)/(kT))`, and another based on a conservation law, an alternative model or a limiting case. If the predictions disagree, do not average the answers. Resolve whether there is a sign error, a unit mistake, a different operating region, or an inaccurate assumption. The specified relation models one aspect of **junction behavior**, not every physical detail.\n\nChallenge the nominal circuit by varying **temperature, geometry and doping**. Explain which quantity should increase and which should decrease based on **microscopic mechanism**. The fact that a classroom formula gives an answer does not establish that a component can deliver the required power or survive the heat. In this example, an important warning is: **The nondegenerate Boltzmann expression is unreliable for heavy degenerate doping and may require Fermi\u2013Dirac statistics.** Specify what extra model parameters would be necessary to remove that uncertainty.\n\nFor an empirical check, **Sketch equilibrium band edges and identify which gradient corresponds to an electric field.** Choose at least five observations over the safe input range, label instruments and units, and record what was controlled rather than inferred. A data point that differs from the model is useful: first test the instrument's loading and connections, then check thermal drift and component tolerances. Explain which observed trend would falsify the original assumption. This is more informative than reporting a nominal error percentage without an uncertainty budget.\n\n\n## Exercises (attempt before consulting references)\n\n1. Describe the carrier, field or feedback mechanism underlying `n\u2248Nc exp(-(Ec-Ef)/(kT))` and show why the predicted output has its stated sign.\n2. Reproduce the numerical case above using SI units, and calculate the percent change in output when the dominant input parameter rises by 20%.\n3. Identify the operating-region or frequency assumption and test one boundary where the formula would be misleading.\n4. Construct a five-point measurement or numerical sweep based on **sketch equilibrium band edges and identify which gradient corresponds to an electric field.**; give axes, expected trend and uncertainty.\n5. Propose a modification that reduces sensitivity to **temperature, geometry and doping**. Which cost, efficiency, gain or bandwidth tradeoff does it create?\n6. Compare a nominal condition and a defensible worst case, and state which additional device-specific data would be required before hardware release.\n\n**Review standard.** A complete solution includes a referenced schematic, units, at least one independent plausibility check, and a clearly separated statement of prediction versus observation. Do not certify a real circuit or safety-critical product solely from these simplified cases.\n"
