          "id": "sec-8-4",
          "secNumber": "8.4",
          "heading": "Output-stage efficiency and thermal path",
          "content": "# Unit 8, Lesson 4: Output-stage efficiency and thermal path\n\n**Model:** `\u03b7B,ideal=\u03c0 Vpk/(4Vs)`  \n**Outcome:** Analyze the operating principle, calculate a reference case, identify the limit of the approximation, and plan an independent check.\n\n## Physical mechanism\n\nFor an ideal class-B complementary push-pull stage on symmetric rails, average DC supply current follows the rectified load current and efficiency approaches \u03c0/4 at full allowable sinusoidal swing. At lower amplitude, the theoretical efficiency is proportionally smaller. Heat is the difference between electrical input and delivered signal power in the simplified model.\n\nDraw the relevant circuit or band diagram and name the source of energy, the signal that controls the result, the return path and the reference directions. A model of an individual element must be used with conservation of current and voltage around the complete circuit. This distinction is critical because a device characteristic measured under one set of bias conditions may not hold with a different load. Describe which parameters are external controls and which are intrinsic or empirical device parameters. In **power stages, interfaces and robust analog design**, an especially important issue is **thermal and safety margins**; make it explicit before calculating anything.\n\n## Derivation and calculation\n\nStart from `\u03b7B,ideal=\u03c0 Vpk/(4Vs)`. Explain the assumptions required to use the expression and distinguish DC current, incremental gain, instantaneous waveform, average power and RMS quantities wherever they arise. Write the associated Kirchhoff equations and translate all input data to compatible SI units. Verify that the final expression has the dimensions of the required output. If the expression is nonlinear, first identify the valid region; if it is an incremental result, identify the linearization point. An elegant equation is not proof that the circuit's bias or signal swing permits the result.\n\n**Worked example.** At Vpk=8 V, symmetric rails \u00b110 V and an 8 \u03a9 load, Pout=4 W and ideal DC input is 2Vs(Vpk/RL)/\u03c0\u22486.37 W, giving \u03b7\u224862.8%.\n\n**Two independent checks.** First examine the zero-drive or zero-load limit where physically applicable. Second change one stated parameter by 10% while holding the others fixed. Explain the sign of the resulting response from the device physics rather than exclusively from differentiation. If this relation predicts a very large current, voltage gain or output power, inspect the supply, load and dissipation limits. The provided numbers are illustrative and are not claims of verified measured devices.\n\n## Limitations and expected departures\n\nReal output voltage headroom, quiescent bias, device drops, thermal coupling and load reactance lower or modify performance.\n\nThis is the boundary of the teaching model, not evidence that all equations are unreliable. To judge model quality, identify the dominant neglected effect and whether it becomes important at high frequency, high drive, high temperature, or a specific operating region. Component spread may require using lower and upper device ratings rather than a single nominal value. Record whether an observed deviation is likely to arise from a different bias condition, instrument impedance or a real phenomenon the simple expression omits.\n\n## Experimental reasoning\n\nSweep output peak from 10% to 90% of supply and estimate output and heat under the ideal model.\n\nBefore energizing hardware, calculate expected terminal voltages and resistor power dissipation and confirm the fixture uses an isolated current-limited low-voltage supply. Measure from a defined ground and avoid exposing test equipment to uncontrolled potentials. Build a table of controlled inputs, model predictions, observed outputs and measurement uncertainty. A measured curve should have labelled axes and should reveal when the hypothesized relation stops being useful. A single matching value does not validate the whole operating range.\n\n## Design analysis and independent validation\n\nTreat **output-stage efficiency and thermal path** as a testable physical claim about **thermal and safety margins**. Before selecting components, define the source, load, reference arrows and nominal temperature. Then make two different predictions: one based on `\u03b7B,ideal=\u03c0 Vpk/(4Vs)`, and another based on a conservation law, an alternative model or a limiting case. If the predictions disagree, do not average the answers. Resolve whether there is a sign error, a unit mistake, a different operating region, or an inaccurate assumption. The specified relation models one aspect of **systems integration**, not every physical detail.\n\nChallenge the nominal circuit by varying **loads, component spread and heat paths**. Explain which quantity should increase and which should decrease based on **failure containment**. The fact that a classroom formula gives an answer does not establish that a component can deliver the required power or survive the heat. In this example, an important warning is: **Real output voltage headroom, quiescent bias, device drops, thermal coupling and load reactance lower or modify performance.** Specify what extra model parameters would be necessary to remove that uncertainty.\n\nFor an empirical check, **Sweep output peak from 10% to 90% of supply and estimate output and heat under the ideal model.** Choose at least five observations over the safe input range, label instruments and units, and record what was controlled rather than inferred. A data point that differs from the model is useful: first test the instrument's loading and connections, then check thermal drift and component tolerances. Explain which observed trend would falsify the original assumption. This is more informative than reporting a nominal error percentage without an uncertainty budget.\n\n\n## Exercises (attempt before consulting references)\n\n1. Describe the carrier, field or feedback mechanism underlying `\u03b7B,ideal=\u03c0 Vpk/(4Vs)` and show why the predicted output has its stated sign.\n2. Reproduce the numerical case above using SI units, and calculate the percent change in output when the dominant input parameter rises by 20%.\n3. Identify the operating-region or frequency assumption and test one boundary where the formula would be misleading.\n4. Construct a five-point measurement or numerical sweep based on **sweep output peak from 10% to 90% of supply and estimate output and heat under the ideal model.**; give axes, expected trend and uncertainty.\n5. Propose a modification that reduces sensitivity to **loads, component spread and heat paths**. Which cost, efficiency, gain or bandwidth tradeoff does it create?\n6. Compare a nominal condition and a defensible worst case, and state which additional device-specific data would be required before hardware release.\n\n**Review standard.** A complete solution includes a referenced schematic, units, at least one independent plausibility check, and a clearly separated statement of prediction versus observation. Do not certify a real circuit or safety-critical product solely from these simplified cases.\n"
